# Copy this to .env for local `netlify dev` testing only. The DEPLOYED site's
# TRC_SERVERS is set in the Netlify UI (Site configuration -> Environment
# variables) and reaches Functions at deploy time — see README.md.
#
# TRC_SERVERS is one JSON object, one entry per registered server id:
#   { "<server_id>": { "w": "<sha256 of that server's write/PushToken>",
#                       "r": "<sha256 of that server's read/HttpApiToken>" } }
#
# - server_id: [a-z0-9-]{1,32}
# - w and r: 64 lowercase hex characters each (a sha256 digest)
# - An id that fails the charset, or a w/r that is not 64 hex characters, is
#   ignored (one console line, no value logged). A registry where two ids
#   share the same w is rejected at load entirely.
#
# Generate a token (do this once per server, for BOTH the write token
# (PushToken) and the read token (HttpApiToken) — they must be different
# random values):
#
#   bash / WSL / macOS:
#     openssl rand -hex 24
#
#   PowerShell:
#     -join ((1..24) | ForEach-Object { "{0:x2}" -f (Get-Random -Max 256) })
#
# Hash a token to get the value that goes in TRC_SERVERS (never store the
# plaintext token anywhere in this repo or in TRC_SERVERS itself — only its
# sha256):
#
#   bash / WSL / macOS (no trailing newline matters — printf, not echo):
#     printf '%s' "$TOKEN" | sha256sum | awk '{print $1}'
#
#   PowerShell:
#     $bytes = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($Token)
#     -join ([System.Security.Cryptography.SHA256]::Create().ComputeHash($bytes) |
#       ForEach-Object { $_.ToString("x2") })
#
# The two placeholder hashes below are NOT real hashes — they are 64
# lowercase hex characters of filler so the shape is obvious. Replace both
# per server, for every server you register.

TRC_SERVERS={"storm10":{"w":"1111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111","r":"2222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222"}}
